\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Dataset split sizes for the three benchmarks.}}{36}{table.caption.34}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Training configuration for the current main distillation script.}}{39}{table.caption.37}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Baseline groups used in the evaluation chapter.}}{40}{table.caption.38}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Original benchmark results of the two SmolVLM students evaluated with VLM-Eval-Kit. These scores define the reference point that all later fine-tuning and distillation gains should be measured against.\relax }}{43}{table.caption.39}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Original benchmark results of the teacher and large reference models used in this thesis. The table shows that the selected teachers cover a wide performance range, with several models far above the compact SmolVLM students.\relax }}{44}{table.caption.40}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces TextVQA comparison across original models, fine-tuning, distillation baselines and GRACE. The main question here is whether adaptive multi-teacher weighting improves over same-family KD and over static multi-teacher baselines.\relax }}{45}{table.caption.41}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces DocVQA comparison across original models, fine-tuning, distillation baselines and GRACE. The table is most useful for checking whether the same teacher pool helps more when document layout and OCR quality matter strongly.\relax }}{46}{table.caption.42}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces ChartQA comparison across original models, fine-tuning, distillation baselines and GRACE. The strongest gain appears here, which suggests that adaptive teacher weighting is especially useful when numerical reasoning and chart reading are combined.\relax }}{47}{table.caption.43}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.6}{\ignorespaces Absolute and relative gains of GRACE{} on SmolVLM-500M. The strongest non-GRACE{} competitor is reinforced selection on TextVQA, uniform multi-teacher weighting on DocVQA and SHM-CKA on ChartQA. The gains over direct finetuning remain large, which shows that the improvement does not come from extra optimization alone but from the distillation and weighting strategy itself.\relax }}{48}{table.caption.45}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.7}{\ignorespaces Gap-to-teacher analysis for SmolVLM-500M under GRACE. The teacher reference is the strongest model inside the four-teacher GRACE pool for each benchmark. GRACE{} closes about half of the original gap on TextVQA and ChartQA, and about 40\% on DocVQA, which shows that the student is not only better in absolute terms but also materially closer to teacher-level performance.\relax }}{49}{table.caption.46}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.8}{\ignorespaces Teacher-assignment study on SmolVLM-500M. Moving from one teacher to a heterogeneous pool already helps, but the strongest gains come from the GRACE weighting rule rather than from teacher count alone. The table isolates teacher assignment from student size, so the difference is easier to attribute to the weighting strategy itself.\relax }}{51}{table.caption.49}%
